intro.bodyMarkdown:
Timers with callbacks work, but they get messy once one step has to wait for another. JavaScript has a better tool: a **promise**. A promise is an object that stands for a value you don't have **yet**: a file being read, data on its way from a server, a timer still counting.

A promise is in one of three states: **pending** (still waiting), **fulfilled** (it worked, and has a value) or **rejected** (it failed, and has an error, the reason). Once it is fulfilled or rejected it is **settled**, and it never changes again.

You make one with `new Promise`, giving it a function that receives two functions of its own: call **`resolve(value)`** when the work succeeds, or **`reject(error)`** when it fails.

```
const coinToss = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    resolve("heads")
  }, 200)
})

coinToss.then((side) => console.log("It landed", side))
console.log("Tossing…")
```

You don't read a promise's value directly. You ask it to call you back with **`.then(fn)`**: when it is fulfilled, `fn` gets the value. **`.catch(fn)`** is called instead if it is rejected.

A very useful promise is one that just waits. This little helper comes back again and again in this quest:

```
function wait(ms) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms))
}

wait(300).then(() => console.log("300 ms later"))
```

Most of the time you won't write `new Promise` yourself: browsers and libraries give you functions that **return** promises (like `fetch`, later in this quest). But knowing how one is made shows you what is going on. `Promise.resolve(value)` and `Promise.reject(error)` make one that is already settled.

